{"name":"Polaris Blue","hex":"#6f8a8c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"polaris","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of polari"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/polaris"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Polaris","blocks":[{"text":"Polaris is a yellow supergiant star in the northern circumpolar constellation of Ursa Minor. It is designated α Ursae Minoris and is commonly called the North Star. With an apparent magnitude that fluctuates around 1.98, it is the brightest star in the constellation and is readily visible to the naked eye at night. The position of the star lies less than 1° away from the north celestial pole, making it the current northern pole star. The stable position of the star in the Northern Sky makes it useful for navigation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Ursa_Minor_constellation_map.svg/330px-Ursa_Minor_constellation_map.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polaris","wikidata":"Q12980"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg/500px-Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg/1280px-Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg/500px-Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Polaris components as seen by the Hubble Space Telescope","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/AlphaUMiLightCurve.png/500px-AlphaUMiLightCurve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/AlphaUMiLightCurve.png/1280px-AlphaUMiLightCurve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/AlphaUMiLightCurve.png/500px-AlphaUMiLightCurve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A light curve for Polaris, plotted from TESS data[29]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlphaUMiLightCurve.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.